<!-- A framed picture on the hall's right-hand wall, where the 2009 view has one
     between the window and the door. Small on purpose: at 0.9 m it is the size
     of a hung print, and a wall-sized version of the same image is a mural,
     which is a different thing to have paid for.

     Turned by a quarter turn about the vertical axis, so the card's +Z normal
     points at -X, into the room a visitor crosses in front of it. That turn is
     the whole of this card's authorship and the reason it is worth saying so:
     the renderer used to drop every heading it was handed (it built its
     rotation with the angle where vecmath wants the axis, which is an angle of
     zero), so a print authored against the east wall drew facing the north one
     instead, so a 0.9 m card seen along its own face is eight pixels of it on
     screen at thirteen metres. Present, textured, and indistinguishable from
     missing art, which is what this file was blamed for.

     x = 10.85 is 15 cm off the plaster, the same stand-off the north wall's
     notice board and the window have. The hall's inner faces are at |x| = 11.0
     and |z| = 8.0, measured off the mesh's own vertices rather than off the
     .dep bounding box, whose 0.506 is the outer edge of the wall's thickness.
     A card hung at 11.0 exactly is hung inside the wall it illustrates, and the
     wall, being coplanar and drawn after it, wins the depth test.

     (An XML comment may not carry a double hyphen, which is also the story of
     how this file once vanished from the world outright: an unparseable cell is
     not an error, it is one fewer cell.) -->
<poster-cell>
    <name>Framed Picture</name>
    <metadata/>
    <image-uri>wla://wonderland-outpatient-clinic/art/picture.png</image-uri>
    <pixelScaleX>0.0035</pixelScaleX>
    <pixelScaleY>0.0035</pixelScaleY>
    <position-component>
        <translation><x>10.85</x><y>1.7</y><z>-1.5</z></translation>
        <rotation><x>0.0</x><y>1.0</y><z>0.0</z><angle>-1.5707963</angle></rotation>
    </position-component>
</poster-cell>
